{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Cd53yh425pNcgLg8bPxJg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done — all 6 acceptance scenarios pass (the 2 new limit scenarios plus the 4 original reservation ones), and `src/test/`, `provided/`, and `pom.xml` are untouched.\n\n**What I added.** The rule needed one new domain object, `TicketHolding`, which owns a member's ticket count and the `LIMIT = 1` invariant. `TicketService` gained two lines:\n\n```java\nTicketHolding.of(userId, tickets.countByUserId(userId)).claimAnother();\n```\n\n**Why a new object rather than putting the check in the service or in `Ticket`.** The rule needs to know *how many tickets this member holds*. `Ticket` can't be the information expert — a single ticket only knows its own reservation state, and the count is a fact spanning tickets; putting it there would give `Ticket` two change axes. The service can't own it either, because `if (tickets.countByUserId(userId) >= 1) throw ...` is the exact shape `rules.md` §3 forbids under Tell-Don't-Ask — the judgement scatters to every caller that asks. So the object that owns the count owns the rule, and the service tells it `claimAnother()` rather than asking for the number.\n\nI deliberately did **not** add a `TicketLimitPolicy` interface. Running §4's gate: `change-ticket-limit.md` specifies no variant (no per-grade or per-event limits), so `v = 1 + 0 = 1 → DEFER`. A one-implementation strategy is Speculative Generality. `LIMIT` stays a constant; I recorded the trigger that would reopen it in `design-notes.md`.\n\n**On ordering.** The limit check sits next to the user-existence check, since both judge the *requester* — it rejects before the ticket is even fetched. That keeps every rejectable judgement ahead of `charge()`, so \"청구는 일어나지 않는다\" holds by ordering alone, with no compensation logic. Notably `Ticket` didn't have to be opened at all — the reservation rule didn't change — which is evidence the two change axes are actually separate.\n\n**One thing worth flagging.** Your first `mvn test` would have failed with 2 errors from a `point_payment.feature` that exists only in the gitignored `target/test-classes` — a leftover from a different branch (`InsufficientPointException.class` is still in `target/classes` too). Maven doesn't purge stale copied resources, so `mvn test` alone re-runs that phantom feature. `mvn clean test` is green. Nothing in your source tree is wrong, but you may hit this again on branch switches.\n\nI also appended a C2 section to `design-notes.md` (the responsibility table and the `v` count), following the convention the file already established."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":315,"cache_read_input_tokens":70377,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":315},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6c3256f3-a036-4690-9b91-e12fc4adca83","uuid":"e6e5bfe0-fe4b-467e-8586-7096c39d1304","timestamp":"2026-07-16T03:48:09.684Z","request_id":"req_011Cd53yepaF278ABpnuFCdc"}
